00:02:05.859 --> 00:02:08.361
look at to see the details of what's going on.

54
00:02:08.461 --> 00:02:11.030
The import that we're going to use

55
00:02:11.030 --> 00:02:13.967
is just from neo4j_for_adk import the graphdb library.

56
00:02:14.534 --> 00:02:16.102
Let's take a look at those details.

57
00:02:16.803 --> 00:02:18.905
Okay, now we're inside of Neo4J for ADK.

58
00:02:19.372 --> 00:02:21.107
And you can see it starts off pretty simply.

59
00:02:21.107 --> 00:02:23.109
We import some stuff from the operating system.

60
00:02:23.443 --> 00:02:25.044
We of course load up the environment.

61
00:02:25.478 --> 00:02:27.113
And then from Neo4J, the

62
00:02:27.113 --> 00:02:29.015
Python package, we load up the GraphDatabase,

63
00:02:29.015 --> 00:02:31.718
and you also get a Result class definition.

64
00:02:32.085 --> 00:02:34.454
Now, a bunch of these functions that are in here are

65
00:02:34.454 --> 00:02:37.056
just really nice for just wrapping up how you interact with Neo4J

66
00:02:37.056 --> 00:02:39.259
and presenting the results from Neo4J in a

67
00:02:39.259 --> 00:02:41.461
way that Google ADK likes to see things.

68
00:02:42.262 --> 00:02:45.331
One of the key things is that results should come back

69
00:02:45.331 --> 00:02:48.434
either as a dictionary with a status that is a success

70
00:02:48.902 --> 00:02:50.703
or a status that is an error.

71
00:02:50.703 --> 00:02:52.505
These two helper functions help do that.

72
00:02:52.572 --> 00:02:56.309
You can call tool_success or tool_error along with some extra arguments

73
00:02:56.643 --> 00:02:59.546
and actually get a nice formatted result from any kind of call.

74
00:02:59.546 --> 00:03:02.949
To that, we then go ahead and also integrate a helper library.

75
00:03:03.917 --> 00:03:06.920
results that are handled by Google ADK need to be easily persisted.

76
00:03:07.620 --> 00:03:09.222
So this function called here to_python

77
00:03:09.756 --> 00:03:11.624
basically goes through all the different kinds

78
00:03:11.624 --> 00:03:13.459
of results you can get back from

79
00:03:13.459 --> 00:03:15.328
Neo4J and makes them easily serializable in

80
00:03:15.328 --> 00:03:17.197
a nice format for for Google ADK.

81
00:03:17.664 --> 00:03:18.698
You can look at the details if

82
00:03:18.698 --> 00:03:19.866
you want to see what's going on here.

83
00:03:20.767 --> 00:03:22.569
The key part of course is this

84
00:03:22.569 --> 00:03:24.404
function that takes the Neo4J result, you're

85
00:03:24.404 --> 00:03:26.372
passing that in as just a Result.

86
00:03:26.606 --> 00:03:28.174
Basically, calls to the Python package.

87
00:03:28.474 --> 00:03:30.310
Packages all that up into a result

88
00:03:30.310 --> 00:03:32.145
that is easily used by Google ADK.

89
00:03:32.745 --> 00:03:34.247
Each of those helper functions

90
00:03:34.681 --> 00:03:38.284
is then also used by this upper level class called Neo4jForADK.

91
00:03:38.952 --> 00:03:41.421
And this is really a wrapper that combines

92
00:03:41.421 --> 00:03:44.190
a Neo4J driver, initializes the environment variables used there,

93
00:03:44.424 --> 00:03:45.992
allows access to the driver,

94
00:03:46.159 --> 00:03:48.728
and then has a special function here for sending queries

95
00:03:49.095 --> 00:03:51.331
and it'll send any kind of query you'd like to,

96
00:03:51.664 --> 00:03:53.700
but then uses those helper functions to

97
00:03:53.700 --> 00:03:56.035
actually reformatting those results into a nice Google

98
00:03:56.035 --> 00:03:59.672
ADK result. At the end then, because we're just going to

99
00:03:59.672 --> 00:04:01.441
have one driver and one connection that we're using,

100
00:04:01.808 --> 00:04:04.277
we're going to have a singleton of this class that we're use.

101
00:04:04.377 --> 00:04:06.012
This is the graph DB that we're

102
00:04:06.012 --> 00:04:07.614
going to actually expose as a variable

103
00:04:07.814 --> 00:04:09.349
and use that through all the notebooks.

104
00:04:10.149 --> 00:04:12.085
Okay, we took a look at Neo4J for ADK.

105
00:04:12.318 --> 00:04:13.953
Let's continue with the notebook. So

106
00:04:13.953 --> 00:04:15.855
now we've got everything set up, and

107
00:04:15.855 --> 00:04:17.757
we can start to define an agent itself.

108
00:04:18.191 --> 00:04:19.425
Okay, with our Neo4J wrapper ready, let's

109
00:04:19.425 --> 00:04:20.660
go ahead and give it a try.

110
00:04:20.960 --> 00:04:22.428
You can just send in kind

111
00:04:22.428 --> 00:04:24.130
of Neo4J query that you'd like to,

112
00:04:24.130 --> 00:04:26.799
as long as it's valid Cypher.
Here the cypher that we're sending

113
00:04:26.799 --> 00:04:28.601
is a very simple cypher statement that

114
00:04:28.601 --> 00:04:30.403
doesn't do any work other than directly

115
00:04:30.403 --> 00:04:32.305
return a result. Here the result is a string,

116
00:04:32.305 --> 00:04:34.307
Neo4J is Ready!

117
00:04:34.307 --> 00:04:37.443
passing that back aliased as a message variable.

118
00:04:37.443 --> 00:04:39.912
So if we run this and then print the results,

119
00:04:39.912 --> 00:04:42.715
you'll see that it's been packaged up into

120
00:04:42.715 --> 00:04:45.518
this nice little dictionary
with a status success

121
00:04:45.518 --> 00:04:47.520
and then the query result
containing an array

122
00:04:47.520 --> 00:04:50.356
of all the rows returned
from the database.

123
00:04:51.758 --> 00:04:55.028
Great, with OpenAI ready and Neo4J ready,
we can start to define

124
00:04:55.028 --> 00:04:58.865
find our tools our tools themselves
and the agent eventually.

125
00:04:59.432 --> 00:05:02.235
Tool definition is an important part
in actually defining an agent.

126
00:05:02.635 --> 00:05:04.370
Without tools, an agent can do a

127
00:05:04.370 --> 00:05:06.139
lot of thinking,
maybe have some chats,

128
00:05:06.139 --> 00:05:07.540
but it can't really do any interactions with

129
00:05:07.540 --> 00:05:08.941
the environment or with the world around it.

130
00:05:09.575 --> 00:05:11.844
So we're going to define a very simple tool just to

131
00:05:11.844 --> 00:05:14.113
kind of illustrate
what kind of things an agent can do.

132
00:05:14.814 --> 00:05:16.549
Hello world is the classic of course.

133
00:05:16.649 --> 00:05:18.451
So we're going to develop the hello

134
00:05:18.451 --> 00:05:20.253
world version of for an agent's tool.

135
00:05:20.253 --> 00:05:22.255
and we're going to call it say hello.

136
00:05:22.722 --> 00:05:24.857
So tools can be simply defined as functions.

137
00:05:25.591 --> 00:05:27.060
Here the function is just to say hello.

138
00:05:27.060 --> 00:05:28.695
It takes a single argument, person_name as a string,

139
00:05:28.695 --> 00:05:30.463
and it returns a dictionary.

140
00:05:30.463 --> 00:05:32.498
Of course, that dictionary is the Google ADK

141
00:05:32.498 --> 00:05:34.567
dictionary that likes to see all results in.

142
00:05:35.268 --> 00:05:37.136
And it also has a couple of important parts here.

143
00:05:37.637 --> 00:05:39.405
There's a doc string that's here, which

144
00:05:39.405 --> 00:05:41.140
is always nice when you're writing code.

145
00:05:41.140 --> 00:05:44.544
It's also especially critical
when you're developing tools for Google ADK.

146
00:05:45.111 --> 00:05:47.380
This doc string is what lets Google

147
00:05:47.380 --> 00:05:49.615
ADK understand what actually this tool does.

148
00:05:50.083 --> 00:05:51.918
This gets passed along to the LLM

149
00:05:51.918 --> 00:05:54.053
when the LLM is told,
here's all the

150
00:05:54.053 --> 00:05:56.155
all the tools
that are available and also

151
00:05:56.155 --> 00:05:58.224
here's what those tools are described as doing.

152
00:05:58.725 --> 00:06:00.860
So, this is a couple of
important parts that we

153
00:06:00.860 --> 00:06:02.995
should go through here.
You describe what the tool does.

154
00:06:02.995 --> 00:06:06.065
Here, it just formats a welcome message
to a named person.

155
00:06:06.065 --> 00:06:07.967
Of course, passed in as person_name.

156
00:06:08.167 --> 00:06:09.602
You describe the arguments that are

157
00:06:09.602 --> 00:06:11.237
being passed in and also the result.

158
00:06:11.571 --> 00:06:14.107
The result here is consistent
through all the tools because

159
00:06:14.107 --> 00:06:16.909
the result is a dictionary
that Google ADK likes to see.

160
00:06:17.410 --> 00:06:18.711
But you can add a little

161
00:06:18.711 --> 00:06:20.213
extra detail if you want to here.

162
00:06:20.213 --> 00:06:21.214
For instance, if you know the

163
00:06:21.214 --> 00:06:22.348
kinds of errors that might come back,

164
00:06:22.648 --> 00:06:24.684
you could explain that here as well.

165
00:06:24.684 --> 00:06:26.919
Inside, the function is incredibly simple.

166
00:06:26.919 --> 00:06:29.055
All we're doing is using Neo4J

167
00:06:29.055 --> 00:06:31.491
again to actually do a hello world.

168
00:06:31.791 --> 00:06:34.460
We're going to call out to Neo4J,
sending a query

169
00:06:34.460 --> 00:06:37.163
where we're still going
to return a hello to you,

170
00:06:37.697 --> 00:06:40.533
but then we're also going
to concatenate that with this person_name,

171
00:06:40.600 --> 00:06:43.369
which is passed in as
an argument to the function.

172
00:06:43.636 --> 00:06:45.204
That's going to get passed in

173
00:06:45.204 --> 00:06:47.039
as a query parameter to this query.

174
00:06:47.540 --> 00:06:50.443
Whenever you see that dollar sign
in a Neo4J Cypher statement,

175
00:06:50.443 --> 00:06:52.478
that means here's a query parameter.

176
00:06:53.045 --> 00:06:54.480
So, here in this array,

177
00:06:54.480 --> 00:06:55.915
in this dictionary that's being passed in.

178
00:06:55.915 --> 00:06:58.951
you'll see the person_name is being
passed in as person_name.

179
00:06:59.318 --> 00:07:01.954
That'll get substituted in as
this variable here, not as

180
00:07:01.954 --> 00:07:04.590
a template substitution,
but as the value gets passed in.

181
00:07:04.924 --> 00:07:06.826
and then this
whole query will get executed.

182
00:07:07.260 --> 00:07:08.761
So let's define that function. And

183
00:07:08.761 --> 00:07:10.496
of course, because this function is just

184
00:07:10.496 --> 00:07:12.398
a normal function, we can try to run it.

185
00:07:12.732 --> 00:07:15.134
So we can print saying hello to ABK

186
00:07:15.601 --> 00:07:20.273
and expect to see a very nice
Google ADK formatted result

187
00:07:20.273 --> 00:07:22.542
that was successful
where the career result that was

188
00:07:22.542 --> 00:07:26.212
sent to Neo4J has the reply,
Hello to you, ABK.

189
00:07:26.679 --> 00:07:28.481
doing that concatenation. Fantastic.

190
00:07:29.048 --> 00:07:30.683
Now just a side note here about

191
00:07:30.683 --> 00:07:32.552
query parameters, part of why we're doing that,

192
00:07:32.552 --> 00:07:35.288
this is good discipline
for any kind of query language that

193
00:07:35.288 --> 00:07:38.291
you're using. Most query languages have the ability
to have query parameters.

194
00:07:38.891 --> 00:07:40.193
And one of the reasons you do

195
00:07:40.193 --> 00:07:41.461
that is to avoid injection attacks, right?

196
00:07:42.061 --> 00:07:44.197
So that people can't pass in values

197
00:07:44.197 --> 00:07:46.666
that look like SQL statements
or Cypher statements

198
00:07:47.200 --> 00:07:49.502
or any other query language statement as a

199
00:07:49.502 --> 00:07:51.804
value and have that
concatenated into a string.

200
00:07:52.038 --> 00:07:53.806
that can cause all kinds of mayhem.

201
00:07:53.806 --> 00:07:55.775
So instead you pass in a query parameter,

202
00:07:56.175 --> 00:07:58.845
and here for instance if I pass in the name and

203
00:07:58.845 --> 00:08:01.781
say, actually the name's going to be,
hey, return some string here,

204
00:08:01.981 --> 00:08:03.850
this could be some malicious code instead

205
00:08:03.850 --> 00:08:05.985
that gets passed in as the person's name.

206
00:08:06.285 --> 00:08:08.087
If you just concatenated that as a string,

207
00:08:08.254 --> 00:08:09.889
all kinds of bad things can happen.

208
00:08:10.323 --> 00:08:11.958
Instead because it's a query parameter,

209
00:08:12.425 --> 00:08:13.993
that still just behaves as a variable.

210
00:08:14.494 --> 00:08:16.195
It gets passed in, it gets concatenated

211
00:08:16.195 --> 00:08:17.897
as a string with the other string,

212
00:08:18.264 --> 00:08:21.567
and the result is simply hello to the somewhat

213
00:08:21.567 --> 00:08:23.769
friendly malicious injection attack.

214
00:08:23.769 --> 00:08:25.638
and of course, it's been avoided now.

215
00:08:26.806 --> 00:08:28.875
We have a basic tool available, now we can

216
00:08:28.875 --> 00:08:30.943
define an agent that's going to use that tool.

217
00:08:31.577 --> 00:08:33.446
Now there are a couple of components of

218
00:08:33.446 --> 00:08:35.348
defining an agent, it's common across all frameworks.

219
00:08:35.548 --> 00:08:37.650
They'll do some variations of the same idea.

220
00:08:37.650 --> 00:08:39.452
Let's take a look at what's happening

221
00:08:39.452 --> 00:08:41.521
inside of Google ADK to define an agent.

222
00:08:44.123 --> 00:08:45.992
Now if you recall earlier, we had

223
00:08:45.992 --> 00:08:47.860
imported this Agent class from Google ADK.

224
00:08:48.394 --> 00:08:49.829
So we're going to make an instance of

225
00:08:49.829 --> 00:08:51.264
that class and it takes a bunch of

226
00:08:51.264 --> 00:08:53.933
parameters. So the first thing that's important is

227
00:08:53.933 --> 00:08:55.968
that every agent is given a name.

228
00:08:55.968 --> 00:08:58.004
Here we're going to call it hello_agent_v1.

229
00:08:58.471 --> 00:09:00.740
This is in case you have multiple versions of the

230
00:09:00.740 --> 00:09:03.009
agent that you want to have at the same time,

231
00:09:03.342 --> 00:09:05.945
or if you want to actually have
different version instructions as well.

232
00:09:06.612 --> 00:09:08.548
Having an idea of what the version of this

233
00:09:08.548 --> 00:09:10.516
agent is is very helpful for debugging later on.

234
00:09:11.350 --> 00:09:13.352
You also pass in the model that you want to use.

235
00:09:13.886 --> 00:09:15.888
If you recall, we defined LLM before

236
00:09:15.888 --> 00:09:17.857
is being a call through LightLLM

237
00:09:18.724 --> 00:09:20.560
out to open AI. So we're going to use that

238
00:09:20.560 --> 00:09:21.994
here. and these next two are pretty

239
00:09:21.994 --> 00:09:23.396
critical for how the Google ADK actually

240
00:09:23.396 --> 00:09:27.400
orchestrates and executes agents.

241
00:09:28.401 --> 00:09:30.469
The first part is this description of the agent.

242
00:09:30.603 --> 00:09:32.939
This is a little bit like the doc string

243
00:09:32.939 --> 00:09:35.575
of the tool, what describes what does this agent do.

244
00:09:35.908 --> 00:09:38.044
This lets Google ADK and also the other

245
00:09:38.044 --> 00:09:40.413
agents understand what is the purpose of this agent

246
00:09:40.413 --> 00:09:42.648
and is there occasions when I should

247
00:09:42.648 --> 00:09:45.217
call this agent rather than doing something myself.

248
00:09:45.618 --> 00:09:47.553
What's called agent delegation.

249
00:09:48.387 --> 00:09:49.622
So that's for other agents to know what

250
00:09:49.622 --> 00:09:50.823
to do with the agent here.

251
00:09:50.823 --> 00:09:53.793
Then for the agent itself,
you provide some instruction.

252
00:09:53.793 --> 00:09:55.895
And the instruction is basically
the same kind of thing

253
00:09:55.895 --> 00:09:58.230
you would have done with uh
when you're doing prompt engineering

254
00:09:58.230 --> 00:10:02.034
and you're defining
the system prompt for uh the LLM.

255
00:10:02.034 --> 00:10:04.637
This is the same kind of thing
that you would do there.

256
00:10:05.171 --> 00:10:06.606
This is a hello world agent,

257
00:10:06.606 --> 00:10:08.307
so it is, of course, very helpful.

258
00:10:08.307 --> 00:10:11.177
It's a helpful assistant that's going to chat with the user

259
00:10:11.177 --> 00:10:13.679
and it really only has one tool
that it wants to use.

260
00:10:13.679 --> 00:10:16.282
So you describe that tool here
so that the agent understands,

261
00:10:16.282 --> 00:10:19.552
not just from the tool definition,
but also from your

262
00:10:19.552 --> 00:10:21.187
instructions that you're giving it,
how to think

263
00:10:21.187 --> 00:10:22.989
about that tool and
when to use that tool.

264
00:10:23.689 --> 00:10:25.625
So here if the user provides their

265
00:10:25.625 --> 00:10:27.560
name, you know, use the say_hello tool

266
00:10:29.328 --> 00:10:31.030
to give them a custom greeting.

267
00:10:34.066 --> 00:10:35.134
And then the final part, of course,

268
00:10:35.134 --> 00:10:36.335
is we have to make those tools available.

269
00:10:36.702 --> 00:10:38.571
The tools get passed in as an

270
00:10:38.571 --> 00:10:40.439
array of just the function names
themselves.

271
00:10:40.439 --> 00:10:41.974
So we're going to pass in say_hello,

272
00:10:42.508 --> 00:10:44.610
and then the agent
then has access to that tool.

273
00:10:44.877 --> 00:10:46.979
It knows what to do
with that tool because

274
00:10:46.979 --> 00:10:49.081
we gave it instructions
and other agents if there are

275
00:10:49.081 --> 00:10:52.184
other agents know
what this agent's role is.

276
00:10:53.185 --> 00:10:55.121
Now that we have an agent
and the agent has the

277
00:10:55.121 --> 00:10:57.023
tool and it has instructions
about how to use

278
00:10:57.023 --> 00:10:58.924
that tool,
now we need to run the agent.

279
00:10:59.358 --> 00:11:02.862
This is the final part
in creating a very basic agentic system.

280
00:11:03.763 --> 00:11:05.965
An agent has to have an execution environment.

281
00:11:06.265 --> 00:11:07.900
So if you look at this diagram

282
00:11:07.900 --> 00:11:09.535
here, you'll see that inner box here.

283
00:11:10.403 --> 00:11:12.104
This is the execution environment that happens.

284
00:11:12.171 --> 00:11:15.408
There's a Runner class that actually
manages all the event

285
00:11:15.408 --> 00:11:18.644
loops basically for calling out to the LLM, passing results.

286
00:11:18.644 --> 00:11:21.047
from the LLM to particular agents and coordinating

287
00:11:21.047 --> 00:11:23.449
all the agents themselves
and how they execute.

288
00:11:23.949 --> 00:11:26.786
Each of those Runners also has access
to various Services,

289
00:11:27.086 --> 00:11:30.423
whether that is in memory services
for like doing actual memory,

290
00:11:30.423 --> 00:11:32.725
memory could be in memory or
it could be, you

291
00:11:32.725 --> 00:11:35.061
could use a database
like Neo4J for memory as well.

292
00:11:35.294 --> 00:11:37.396
that gets you access to Storage.

293
00:11:37.396 --> 00:11:40.433
Then there's the Execution Logic
that inside of the execution loop, here

294
00:11:40.433 --> 00:11:43.469
are the steps of for actually running things
and coordinating the agent,

295
00:11:43.469 --> 00:11:44.970
whether agents are running in parallel

296
00:11:44.970 --> 00:11:48.340
or in sequence or in a loop. This all together

297
00:11:48.340 --> 00:11:50.576
that are actually is the execution environment for the agent.

298
00:11:51.210 --> 00:11:53.646
Okay. We're going to do this
by hand first and then

299
00:11:53.646 --> 00:11:56.282
show how we can wrap this up
to a little convenience method.

300
00:11:56.816 --> 00:11:58.517
When you're doing your own development
with Google ADK,

301
00:11:59.285 --> 00:12:02.354
Google ADK has really great tooling
for just defining an agent

302
00:12:02.621 --> 00:12:04.657
and then using the tooling for actually
just running that agent,

303
00:12:04.657 --> 00:12:07.593
providing it the full execution environment
that it needs.

304
00:12:07.860 --> 00:12:10.029
We're going to be doing that
by hand inside of the notebook.

305
00:12:10.429 --> 00:12:11.964
So let's do that one step at a time

306
00:12:11.964 --> 00:12:13.499
and then wrap it up into a little helper.

307
00:12:15.101 --> 00:12:16.235
So there are a couple things you're

308
00:12:16.235 --> 00:12:17.570
going to need to actually set up the

309
00:12:17.570 --> 00:12:21.474
execution environment.
Every runtime actually runs within a session.

310
00:12:21.807 --> 00:12:23.576
You could of course have multiple sessions

311
00:12:23.576 --> 00:12:25.177
that are running in some production environment.

312
00:12:25.177 --> 00:12:27.012
Here we're going to have a single session.

313
00:12:27.246 --> 00:12:29.181
we're going to go ahead and just set

314
00:12:29.181 --> 00:12:31.150
up the in memory service which provides context

315
00:12:31.350 --> 00:12:33.252
and state for the agent as it's running.

316
00:12:34.053 --> 00:12:37.022
We're going to create the session service
based on that memory

317
00:12:37.389 --> 00:12:39.458
and also for the particular agent
that we're writing.

318
00:12:39.625 --> 00:12:41.160
And also assuming
that we have a user, we're

319
00:12:41.160 --> 00:12:42.661
going to assume that we have a single user,

320
00:12:43.028 --> 00:12:45.831
a single application
that we're running and a single session.

321
00:12:46.699 --> 00:12:48.968
All that is going to be used here in the create_session

322
00:12:48.968 --> 00:12:51.670
to actually create a single executable state

323
00:12:51.670 --> 00:12:53.873
to actually be run and then the runner

324
00:12:53.873 --> 00:12:57.777
is going to run that state given
the agent that it has.

325
00:12:57.777 --> 00:12:59.678
So here the runner combines the agent that

326
00:12:59.678 --> 00:13:01.580
we want to run, our hello world agent.

327
00:13:01.580 --> 00:13:03.115
The app_name is going to be the

328
00:13:03.115 --> 00:13:04.884
same as we passed into the session here.

329
00:13:04.884 --> 00:13:06.819
And also the session_service is going to

330
00:13:06.819 --> 00:13:09.088
be the session within which this agent runs.

331
00:13:09.088 --> 00:13:10.956
Again in a production environment,

332
00:13:10.956 --> 00:13:12.892
there might be multiple sessions,
there might

333
00:13:12.892 --> 00:13:15.060
be multiple applications
that are using this agent.

334
00:13:15.060 --> 00:13:17.396
That's why these are all different
from arguments to run.

335
00:13:19.031 --> 00:13:21.100
Okay, now there's a lot to walk through to

336
00:13:21.100 --> 00:13:23.369
just actually do the running of the agent as well.

337
00:13:23.369 --> 00:13:25.371
So let's go through this step by step
and take our time.

338
00:13:26.005 --> 00:13:27.072
We know that we just have

339
00:13:27.072 --> 00:13:28.340
this runner available that we just defined.

340
00:13:28.707 --> 00:13:30.509
And we're going to do just a

341
00:13:30.509 --> 00:13:32.545
single loop through with a single user message

342
00:13:32.545 --> 00:13:34.647
and allow the runner to execute the

343
00:13:34.647 --> 00:13:37.049
agent reacting to that message from the user.

344
00:13:37.750 --> 00:13:39.518
So we're going to define an user message

345
00:13:39.518 --> 00:13:41.520
and I'm simply going to say,
hello, I'm ABK.

346
00:13:42.288 --> 00:13:44.089
And we'll go ahead and
print that message out

347
00:13:44.089 --> 00:13:45.791
in a friendly way so we ourselves can

348
00:13:45.791 --> 00:13:47.526
see as this is running what's going on.

349
00:13:48.060 --> 00:13:50.896
That message that is just
a string has to get packed up

350
00:13:50.896 --> 00:13:54.633
into a data structure that Google ADK
the runtime environment expects.

351
00:13:54.967 --> 00:13:56.435
Usually this is handled
behind the scenes for you

352
00:13:56.435 --> 00:13:58.037
of course, but we're going
to do this by hand.

353
00:13:58.270 --> 00:14:00.940
So we're going to create
this content class here.

354
00:14:01.907 --> 00:14:04.143
and the role of the content
that we're creating

355
00:14:04.143 --> 00:14:06.712
is from the user, this is a user message.

356
00:14:06.979 --> 00:14:08.914
content can have multiple parts.

357
00:14:08.914 --> 00:14:10.783
So we're going to create an array of parts

358
00:14:10.783 --> 00:14:12.618
where one of the parts is has some text

359
00:14:12.618 --> 00:14:15.154
where the text is a user message.

360
00:14:15.654 --> 00:14:18.157
This is all bundled up to do more complex things.

361
00:14:18.157 --> 00:14:19.625
Here we're just trying to run

362
00:14:19.625 --> 00:14:21.327
a single message from a single user,

363
00:14:21.327 --> 00:14:23.929
but this gives you lots of flexibility when

364
00:14:23.929 --> 00:14:26.565
you have lots of extra things going on.

365
00:14:26.565 --> 00:14:28.934
and this is basically creating a content event, but

366
00:14:28.934 --> 00:14:31.570
then has to get processed
by the agent runtime system.

367
00:14:31.570 --> 00:14:33.806
Also, we're going to go ahead and set up

368
00:14:33.806 --> 00:14:35.941
a response from the agent system,

369
00:14:35.941 --> 00:14:38.143
in case it doesn't do anything.

370
00:14:38.143 --> 00:14:40.212
So after you pass in a message,

371
00:14:40.212 --> 00:14:42.748
we're going to actually go through
running that message and

372
00:14:42.748 --> 00:14:45.284
letting the agent get a chance to respond,
but it's

373
00:14:45.284 --> 00:14:46.785
possible that the agent doesn't respond.

374
00:14:47.319 --> 00:14:49.588
There's this notion of having a final response,

375
00:14:50.256 --> 00:14:53.959
which is a flag that an agent can set
in different ways

376
00:14:53.959 --> 00:14:55.527
to actually indicate, okay, I'm done thinking

377
00:14:55.527 --> 00:14:57.096
about this or I'm done processing this.

378
00:14:57.263 --> 00:14:59.265
So you can go on and go back to the user.

379
00:14:59.765 --> 00:15:01.300
In case the agent does nothing,

380
00:15:01.300 --> 00:15:03.102
a final response may not get set.

381
00:15:03.369 --> 00:15:05.537
So we preset it here with a default

382
00:15:05.537 --> 00:15:07.973
value to let us know that nothing has happened.

383
00:15:08.374 --> 00:15:11.477
We're going to run through
without any verbose output right now,

384
00:15:11.777 --> 00:15:13.245
and you can see in a second

385
00:15:13.245 --> 00:15:14.747
what the verbose output might look like.

386
00:15:14.747 --> 00:15:16.815
This is the event loop itself.

387
00:15:17.349 --> 00:15:20.686
well, one step I should say of the event,
you know, for

388
00:15:20.686 --> 00:15:23.989
this one message where given the user message,
we're going to asynchronously

389
00:15:24.456 --> 00:15:26.825
use the runner and call out to the

390
00:15:26.825 --> 00:15:29.495
LLM using the message that was passed in, the

391
00:15:29.495 --> 00:15:31.897
agent that was defined to use that model

392
00:15:31.897 --> 00:15:34.566
and all the instructions that we had given it.

393
00:15:35.067 --> 00:15:36.535
So the first thing that you'll notice

394
00:15:36.535 --> 00:15:37.970
is that when we call this async,

395
00:15:37.970 --> 00:15:39.438
we also pass in all the same

396
00:15:39.438 --> 00:15:40.906
information we had for creating the session.

397
00:15:41.140 --> 00:15:42.508
This is because you can run

398
00:15:42.508 --> 00:15:44.076
things again, there can be multiple sessions.

399
00:15:44.076 --> 00:15:46.545
is going on. So you have multiple event loops running around.

400
00:15:47.046 --> 00:15:48.948
And asynchronously, you pass in here's

401
00:15:48.948 --> 00:15:51.116
the context that I'm currently operating in.

402
00:15:51.116 --> 00:15:53.852
It's for this user in this session

403
00:15:54.219 --> 00:15:57.122
and here's the content that's getting passed in,
go ahead and

404
00:15:57.122 --> 00:16:00.025
execute this once
with the LLM do some work on this.

405
00:16:00.693 --> 00:16:02.661
This is inside of a for

406
00:16:02.661 --> 00:16:04.964
loop because this is an agentic system.

407
00:16:04.964 --> 00:16:07.666
So once the agent is given this message, it might

408
00:16:07.666 --> 00:16:10.636
do a bunch of things before actually
having that final response.

409
00:16:11.203 --> 00:16:13.772
So the event loop here pulls out events

410
00:16:13.772 --> 00:16:16.141
from the asynchronous running of the agent,

411
00:16:17.109 --> 00:16:20.045
every event is some update from the agent itself.

412
00:16:20.813 --> 00:16:23.382
As the events come in, if the verbose mode is turned on,

413
00:16:23.582 --> 00:16:25.351
we'll print out, here's an event that happened,

414
00:16:25.918 --> 00:16:29.722
here's who created that event.
The initial event, of course, should be

415
00:16:29.722 --> 00:16:32.091
that the user message was passed in, so we

416
00:16:32.091 --> 00:16:34.493
should see the event author was the user themselves,

417
00:16:35.194 --> 00:16:37.062
and you'll see the content of the event as well.

418
00:16:37.463 --> 00:16:39.631
You can take a look what's happening here
in the debug statement.

419
00:16:39.999 --> 00:16:41.567
bunch of things get printed out which

420
00:16:41.567 --> 00:16:43.168
are helpful to understand what's going on.

421
00:16:43.902 --> 00:16:44.970
And then this is the key

422
00:16:44.970 --> 00:16:46.205
concept for keeping the event loop running

423
00:16:46.705 --> 00:16:48.674
for as long as you'd like it to run.

424
00:16:48.741 --> 00:16:51.844
Hopefully the agent will run only
as much as it needs to.

425
00:16:52.478 --> 00:16:55.447
But as each event comes in,
there's a flag that says, okay,

426
00:16:55.848 --> 00:16:57.916
this is the final event, I'm done

427
00:16:57.916 --> 00:17:00.019
processing this, the agent is done processing.

428
00:17:00.119 --> 00:17:01.754
If it has set this, then you

429
00:17:01.754 --> 00:17:03.422
know that okay, the processing is over.

430
00:17:03.522 --> 00:17:05.724
You can take a look at the content

431
00:17:05.724 --> 00:17:07.960
and decide what the final response might be.

432
00:17:08.260 --> 00:17:10.329
Here we're taking a look at the content

433
00:17:10.329 --> 00:17:12.364
if they have some multiple parts.

434
00:17:12.598 --> 00:17:15.367
if it's a non-zero array of text that come back,

435
00:17:15.834 --> 00:17:17.269
we're going to take the first

436
00:17:17.269 --> 00:17:18.971
message back from the parts zero here,

437
00:17:19.138 --> 00:17:22.541
grab the text from that.
And here we're making some assumptions

438
00:17:22.541 --> 00:17:24.076
that the first text response in
the first part

439
00:17:24.076 --> 00:17:25.744
is actually the right one.

440
00:17:25.744 --> 00:17:27.780
Turn that into the final response text.

441
00:17:28.247 --> 00:17:30.749
Alternatively, the agent could have decided this is

442
00:17:30.749 --> 00:17:33.285
my final response and by the way, I

443
00:17:33.285 --> 00:17:35.788
don't have actually a response,
what I've asked

444
00:17:35.788 --> 00:17:38.624
the runtime system to do is actually to escalate.

445
00:17:38.891 --> 00:17:40.959
Now here escalation ends up meaning

446
00:17:40.959 --> 00:17:43.095
the agent is running, knows that it's a sub agent

447
00:17:43.095 --> 00:17:44.930
and it can't handle or it can't

448
00:17:44.930 --> 00:17:46.999
do any more work with what's currently available.

449
00:17:47.399 --> 00:17:50.169
So it's basically saying,
hey, escalate this to somebody else,

450
00:17:50.169 --> 00:17:52.838
either my parent or
to some other agent that might be appropriate.

451
00:17:53.539 --> 00:17:55.507
In that case, the final response text that

452
00:17:55.507 --> 00:17:57.476
we're going to set is based on that.

453
00:17:57.476 --> 00:17:59.344
We're going to say that the agent has

454
00:17:59.344 --> 00:18:01.413
decided to escalate the current handling of the message

455
00:18:01.580 --> 00:18:03.282
and then we don't have any

456
00:18:03.282 --> 00:18:05.250
specific message or there's an error message

457
00:18:05.484 --> 00:18:08.654
if there is one there and return that as the final message.

458
00:18:08.821 --> 00:18:11.223
all that is to continue to run through

459
00:18:11.223 --> 00:18:13.692
one round through the event loop,

460
00:18:13.692 --> 00:18:16.562
instigated by a single user message, and then at the end

461
00:18:16.562 --> 00:18:19.131
we'll go ahead and print out the final agent response.

462
00:18:19.465 --> 00:18:20.532
That's kind of a lot to go

463
00:18:20.532 --> 00:18:21.600
through, but keep that in your heads

464
00:18:21.600 --> 00:18:23.802
a little bit as you're going through all the notebooks.

465
00:18:23.936 --> 00:18:26.004
Internally, this is really what's happening to

466
00:18:26.004 --> 00:18:28.040
actually make all the the machinery run.

467
00:18:29.108 --> 00:18:31.577
For all that work, there we go. Simple chat.

468
00:18:31.877 --> 00:18:34.580
Hello, I'm ABK. Hello to you, ABK.

469
00:18:35.514 --> 00:18:36.882
Of course, because we're going to be

470
00:18:36.882 --> 00:18:38.217
doing a lot of calling to agents,

471
00:18:38.217 --> 00:18:41.386
manually. We're going to set up the helper class to

472
00:18:41.386 --> 00:18:43.655
actually let us do that a little bit more easily.

473
00:18:44.556 --> 00:18:47.025
And I'm going to call that helper class AgentCaller.

474
00:18:47.025 --> 00:18:49.795
This AgentCaller basically wraps up those functions that

475
00:18:49.795 --> 00:18:52.931
we went through each one at a time here,

476
00:18:53.899 --> 00:18:56.268
and also the event loop itself

477
00:18:57.536 --> 00:19:01.006
into one class. And if you look through the details,

478
00:19:01.607 --> 00:19:04.076
it is doing all the things that we'd seen above.

479
00:19:04.476 --> 00:19:07.746
It's saving the single user ID, the

480
00:19:07.746 --> 00:19:09.915
single session ID, the current agent
that we're going to be running

481
00:19:09.915 --> 00:19:11.884
and also the runner that's been set up.

482
00:19:12.851 --> 00:19:14.786
It has access to getting the session

483
00:19:14.786 --> 00:19:16.989
that it uses internally for actually managing stuff.

484
00:19:18.457 --> 00:19:21.627
And this call function here is
what actually instigates one round trip

485
00:19:21.627 --> 00:19:24.329
through the event loop based on a user message.

486
00:19:25.564 --> 00:19:27.099
So it does just what we saw

487
00:19:27.099 --> 00:19:28.800
above, takes the user message, packages it up

488
00:19:28.800 --> 00:19:30.569
so that it's available as a Content

489
00:19:30.569 --> 00:19:32.838
to be processed by the agent.

490
00:19:33.405 --> 00:19:35.274
And then it runs through the event loop.

491
00:19:36.041 --> 00:19:38.844
Same kind of debug options available, as well.

492
00:19:39.978 --> 00:19:41.613
And this is really a stylistic choice.

493
00:19:41.613 --> 00:19:43.448
I'm not a native Python developer and so

494
00:19:43.448 --> 00:19:45.317
this is the way I like to do things.

495
00:19:45.317 --> 00:19:47.085
I prefer having factory methods for things

496
00:19:47.085 --> 00:19:49.121
that are a little bit complex to construct.

497
00:19:49.421 --> 00:19:52.324
So rather than having a complex constructor
inside of that class,

498
00:19:52.591 --> 00:19:55.494
I created a separate class
that actually does the construction.

